{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788318787,"nonce":"7OyL6I1/VGOlvtgx52GOpoksRkY=","title":"**Hard blocker for any unattended run, and it fails in the worst possible way.** Hit live in `m1-20260901` at ~tick 1,891,000 (run day 31), mid-overnight.","message":"Every `rwa` call started dying with a Python traceback instead of an envelope:\n\n```\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \".../rwa/rwa\", line 2092, in \u003cmodule\u003e\n    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))\n  File \".../rwa/rwa\", line 2056, in main\n    env = send(root, op, args, opts, h, transcript)\n  File \".../rwa/rwa\", line 805, in send\n    step_dir = transcript.step(op) if transcript else None\n  File \".../rwa/rwa\", line 693, in step\n    raise RuntimeError(\"transcript run is full (1000 steps)\")\n```\n\n`RWA_RUN=m1-20260901` had accumulated 1,000 numbered step directories and the client refuses to allocate a 1,001st. The colony was fine; the game was fine; the *client* was bricked for that run name.\n\n\nThree separate problems, in order of severity:\n\n1. **It is not an envelope.** Every other failure mode in the client produces `{id, op, ok:false, error:{code:\"rwa-…\"}, …}` — that is the stated contract: *\"A client-side failure gets the same shape with an `rwa-…` error code … so a jq pipeline never has to branch on who failed.\"* This one is a bare `RuntimeError` on stderr with exit code 1. Any driver that branches on `.ok` sees empty stdout and cannot tell a full transcript from a dead bench.\n2. **It happens after the command is composed but before it is sent**, so it is silent about the *most useful* fact: nothing was dispatched. That is good behaviour and completely unstated.\n3. **1,000 steps is a few hours of play, not a run.** This run reached the cap on in-game day 31 of an open-ended session. A 20-day run costs roughly 700–1,000 calls at one digest + one journal + one advance + a handful of acts per turn; anything longer than that cannot complete without operator intervention. `664e9b9` explicitly allows a run to span sessions and bench relaunches, and the deliverables ask for \"a complete transcript\" — the artifact the cap destroys.\n\n\nMade the wrapper auto-rotate: pick the highest `${BASE}-sNN` segment with fewer than 940 steps, else start a new one.\n\n```bash\nseg=0\nwhile :; do\n  d=\"$TR/${BASE}-s$(printf %02d $seg)\"\n  n=$( [ -d \"$d\" ] \u0026\u0026 find \"$d\" -maxdepth 1 -type d -name '[0-9]*' | wc -l || echo 0 )\n  [ \"$n\" -lt 940 ] \u0026\u0026 break\n  seg=$((seg+1))\ndone\nexport RWA_RUN=\"${BASE}-s$(printf %02d $seg)\"\n```\n\nThat works, and it is client policy living in a shell script — exactly the kind of thing spec 1.4 says should not be in a caller. It also means the run's transcript is now split across `m1-20260901` and `m1-20260901-sNN`, which `accept/4.2-play-loop.py --transcript` takes as a single directory.\n\n\n1. The 1,001st call **returns an envelope**, not a traceback: `ok:false` with an `rwa-…` code (proposed `rwa-transcript-full`), the run name, the cap, and the fact that **nothing was dispatched**. Every client-side failure already has this shape; this is the one that does not.\n2. **The client rotates by default.** On reaching the cap it starts `\u003crun\u003e-002` (or similar), logs one line saying so, and continues — because the alternative is that a run dies at an arbitrary point for a reason that has nothing to do with the colony. An opt-out (`--no-rotate`) is fine for a scripted scenario that wants exactly one directory.\n3. `meta.json` in each segment names its predecessor and successor so a consumer can walk the chain, and `accept/4.2-play-loop.py --transcript` accepts a glob or a chain rather than one directory. Without this, \"a complete transcript\" is unverifiable for any long run.\n4. The cap itself is re-justified or raised. If it exists to bound directory size, say so in `rwa/README.md` §Transcripts, which currently documents the layout and never mentions a limit.\n5. `selftest.sh` covers it: drive a synthetic root past the cap, assert the envelope shape, assert rotation, assert the chain links.\n\n\nSame family as `bb931b9` (no save verb) and `5cb1f9f` (dialog wedge): three separate ways an unattended run stops for a reason unrelated to the colony. Worth triaging together as \"what does a multi-day run need that a one-day acceptance never exercises\".","files":null}]}